--- /dev/null
+++ b/ROOT-SECURITY-RELEASE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+# CVE-2026-56375 — False Positive for Debian 13 (trixie)
+
+| Field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| **CVE ID** | CVE-2026-56375 |
+| **Component** | imagemagick (ASHLAR coder) |
+| **Distribution** | Debian 13 (trixie) |
+| **Package version** | `imagemagick`, version `8:7.1.1.43+dfsg1-1+deb13u11` |
+| **Upstream affected** | ImageMagick 7.1.2-x through 7.1.2-18 (leak on CompositeImage failure) |
+| **Assessment** | **FALSE POSITIVE — package is not vulnerable, no patch required.** |
+
+## Why this release is not affected
+CVE-2026-56375 is a memory leak in the ASHLAR coder when an action fails.
+Debian's tracker cites upstream commit 3f55da39c813cc44ff21a61d6e0b85c375a2c1a2
+(7.1.2-18), which changes:
+
+    status=CompositeImage(...);
+    if (status == MagickFalse)
+      continue;   /* leaked tile_image */
+
+to destroy `tile_image` before continuing.
+
+Debian trixie ships ImageMagick 7.1.1.43. In that tree, `ASHLARImage()`
+discards the CompositeImage return value (`(void) CompositeImage(...)`)
+and always calls `tile_image=DestroyImage(tile_image)` at the end of the
+tile loop. The early-continue leak path never landed. `WriteASHLARImage()`
+also always destroys `clone_images` after `ASHLARImage()` returns.
+
+Debian marks trixie "vulnerable (no DSA)" by version comparison only
+(fixed upstream in 7.1.2-18). There is no Debian patch for this CVE in
+`8:7.1.1.43+dfsg1-1+deb13u11`.
+
+## Evidence
+- `coders/ashlar.c` in 7.1.1.43: no `if (status == MagickFalse) continue`
+  after CompositeImage; `DestroyImage(tile_image)` is unconditional.
+- Tracker: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-56375
+
+## Recommended action
+- Mark as `not_affected` / `vulnerable_code_not_present` in the scanner.
+
+## References
+- https://github.com/ImageMagick/ImageMagick/commit/3f55da39c813cc44ff21a61d6e0b85c375a2c1a2
+- https://github.com/ImageMagick/ImageMagick/security/advisories/GHSA-6p22-q7w5-33pg
+- https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-56375
